ailing. As at the date of this Offering Circular, Tesco operates retailing businesses through stores and online through two segments, UK/ROI, including the Booker wholesale and catering business, and Central Europe, including Czech Republic, Hungary and Slovakia. The Group includes Tesco Mobile, an award-winning mobile virtual network operator; the Booker Group, the UK’s leading food wholesaler; dunnhumby, a wholly -owned data science subsidiary; One Stop, a retail convenience business focused on providing customers in local communities with quality products and services at competitive prices; and Tesco Insurance and Money Services, which is the second largest provider of travel money in the UK with the third largest ATM network in the UK. In November 2024, the Group completed the sale of its banking operations, comprising personal loans, credit cards, customer deposits, and associated operational capabilities to Barclays Bank UK plc . Following completion of the sale, the Group announced its intention to return £700m to shareholders via an incremental share buyback. The Group also has retail and wholesale interests in India through two Indian joint venture agreements with Trent Limited (part of the Tata Group of companies). Tesco is the overall holding company of the Group. As the holding company of the Group, Tesco is dependent on the performance of its operating subsidiaries and the payment of dividends by them. Strategy The Group maintains a strategic focus on driving growth and focusing on cost reduction from the simplification of its operating model. Its financial priorities are to deliver cash profit, free cash flow and earnings per share growth, underpinned by a robus t capital allocation framework. The Group has a diversified business portfolio covering retail, wholesale , financial services and data science. The Group publishes its approach to being a responsible and sustainable business in its Annual Report and on a dedicated webpage (www.tescoplc.com/sustainability). TCTS is an issuer of ESG-linked bonds, which are aligned to Tesco’s Sustainability Performance Target to reduce Scope 1 and 2 GHG emissions. These topics are important to the Group’s customers but are also essential for the long -term sustainability of the Group’s business. Channels For the 52 weeks ended 22 February, 2025, the Group’s principal activities were reported in the following segments: • the UK and ROI8; and • Central Europe – Czech Republic, Hungary and Slovakia. UK and ROI Segment 8 Following the disposal of the Group’s banking operations, the insurance and money services business previously reported within the Tesco Bank segment has been reclassified to the UK and ROI segment, with comparative segmental reporting restated. 105 As at 22 February, 2025, the Group operated 3,829 9 stores in the United Kingdom and 182 stores in the ROI. These include Tesco Express convenience stores, Tesco superstores, Tesco Extra hypermarkets, One Stop convenience stores and Booker stores. Central Europe Segment As at 22 February, 2025, the Grou p had 184 stores in the Czech Republic 10, 198 in Hungary and 179 in Slovakia11. Board of Directors of Tesco The current officers of Tesco, as at the date of this Offering Circular, are, and their functions and principal activities outside Tesco, where these are significant with respect to the Group, as at the date of this Offering Circular, are, as follows: Name of Officer Role Principal activities outside Tesco Dr Gerry Murphy Chair • Chair of Burberry Group plc; and • Senior advisor to Perella Weinberg Partners. Ken Murphy Group Chief Executive • None. Imran Nawaz Chief Financial Officer • None. Dame Carolyn Fairbairn DBE Senior Independent Director • Non-executive director of HSBC Holdings plc; and • Chair of the board of trustees of Royal Mencap Society. Melissa Bethell Independent Non - executive Director • Non-executive director of Diageo PLC; • Senior adviser to Atairos; • Non-executive director of Exor N.V.;